  "updatedAt": "2026-11-22T14:00:00Z",
  "body": [
    "Royal Holloway, University of London, reached a financial settlement with student Brodie Mitchell in April over his suspension. In a joint statement published April 21, the university said it made a goodwill payment and regretted the length of the suspension and the distress it caused. The settlement included no admission of liability, and Mitchell agreed to drop his claim.",
    "Later that month, Royal Holloway referred Mitchell to Prevent after a separate post on X. Police concluded on June 2 that the case did not meet the counterterrorism threshold. The university’s financial settlement concerned the original suspension. The later referral arose from a new incident.",
    "The suspension and settlement",
    "The first dispute began at a September 2025 Freshers’ Fair. During an exchange with a pro-Palestinian student, Mitchell, an outspoken supporter of Israel, called the other student’s keffiyeh a “tea towel.” The Telegraph reported that Royal Holloway suspended him for nine weeks.",
    "Mitchell acknowledged that his remark was inappropriate and said the university’s response disproportionately interfered with his freedom of expression. Royal Holloway said it was enforcing student conduct rules.",
    "What prompted the Prevent referral",
    "Mitchell said he received death threats and that his address was published online. In an April post about the harassment he reported, he called for the Pakistani Muslims he accused of leaking his address to be sent home. According to reporting on university records, a Royal Holloway panel flagged the post as possible hate speech. The university referred him to Prevent. Mitchell said the referral felt disproportionate and retaliatory.",
    "Prevent assesses whether someone may be susceptible to being drawn into terrorism. Police said in a June 2 email that Mitchell’s case did not meet the criteria for counterterrorism intervention. Home Office guidance calls for a proportionate approach and directs universities to give particular regard to freedom of speech and academic freedom. Royal Holloway declined to discuss the individual case in October reporting and said it takes student welfare, data protection and campus safety seriously.",
    "The Telegraph’s November 21 report added details about the university’s monitoring based on 1,465 pages released through a data-access request. Many pages were heavily redacted. The reported records identify the post that prompted the referral. They leave open what evidence Royal Holloway had that Mitchell was at risk of being drawn into terrorism."
  ],
